{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century. \n*Early Life* \nRaymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem his mother was a sister of Queen Melisende of Jerusalem and a daughter of King Baldwin II of Jerusalem. \n*Regency and Reign* \nRaymond III became the Count of Tripoli in 1152 after the death of his father he was still a minor at that time so the county was governed by a regent. In 1164 Raymond III was taken prisoner by Nur ad-Din the emir of Aleppo during a battle he was released in 1173. \n*Later Life and Death* \nRaymond III died in 1187 without any legitimate male heirs the county of Tripoli was inherited by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. He was a member of the court of King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the politics of the kingdom. \n*Relationships and Alliances* \nRaymond III formed alliances with other Christian leaders in the Holy Land including King Amalric I of Jerusalem and King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem. He also formed alliances with Muslim leaders including Saladin the sultan of Egypt and Syria. \n*Military Campaigns* \nRaymond III participated in several military campaigns against Muslim forces including the Battle of Inab in 1149 and the Battle of al-Babein in 1167. He also participated in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 and the Battle of Hattin in 1187. \n*Legacy* \nRaymond III is remembered as a skilled military leader and a shrewd politician who played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century. He was also known for his bravery and his ability to form alliances with both Christian and Muslim leaders. \n*Family and Succession* \nRaymond III was married to Hodierna of Jerusalem but the marriage was childless after his death the county of Tripoli was inherited by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -4.0531076592742465e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.00043096792069263756}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.00012432756193447858}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.20712825655937195}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.710232675075531}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -5.602820692729438e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.023202933371067047}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": "115", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.0009124883217737079}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": "118", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.10644540935754776}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.007799291517585516}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.026432808488607407}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.025843778625130653}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -0.00012385078298393637}, {"token": " II", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0012978235026821494}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " H", "logprob": -2.253030106658116e-05}, {"token": "odi", "logprob": -0.0008679436868987978}, {"token": "erna", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " of", "lo